          { name: "Treating College-Educated Voters as a Locked Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "Affluent suburban districts with 41.6% college attainment have demonstrated capacity in recent election cycles to split tickets and reward candidates who engage substantive policy details over partisan messaging. Assuming that the Republican sweep represents stable support from the college-educated plurality is strategically dangerous — these voters evaluate on education funding, healthcare policy, and climate-resilient infrastructure investments with the same intensity that other constituencies evaluate on taxes." },
          { name: "Minimizing the District's Demographic Diversity", tag: "avoid", why: "While 70.83% white, MD-HD-7A still contains 13.88% Black, 6.44% Asian, and 5.04% Hispanic residents — meaningful minority communities whose economic concerns and civic participation deserve active engagement rather than default assumptions that they align with the Republican margin. In a district this affluent, minority communities include both professional households and service workers, and both constituencies have direct material interests in education funding, healthcare access, and economic mobility that should be addressed explicitly rather than absorbed into a generic majority narrative." }
