          { name: "Racial Diversity & Specific Community Needs", tag: "lean-into", why: "With no single racial group exceeding 39%, MD-HD-17 is genuinely multiethnic, requiring candidates who can articulate specific policy positions on issues affecting each community — healthcare access and language services for the 23.08% Hispanic population, small business access and educational equity for the 20.45% Asian population, criminal justice and economic mobility for the 13.37% Black population. Candidates who treat this diversity as a monolithic progressive bloc rather than distinct constituencies with distinct needs miss both mobilization opportunities and vulnerability points." },
          { name: "Understanding the Democratic Sweep's Fragility", tag: "careful", why: "A 100% seat takeover in a three-seat at-large district is not typical and suggests either overwhelming partisan preference, superior Democratic execution, or weakness in Republican alternatives — but at-large formats can be volatile if a credible alternative emerges. The Democratic incumbents should understand specifically whether they are holding these seats because the constituency actively prefers them or because opposition is poorly organized; the latter scenario creates vulnerability to a well-resourced challenger or a shift in local conditions." },
          { name: "Assuming the 100% Result Reflects a Stable Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "The complete Democratic sweep should not be interpreted as permanent consolidation; in a 58.9% college-educated, high-income, genuinely diverse district, voter preferences are often negotiable rather than locked. Any incumbent or Democratic candidate who assumes the 100% result means the district is secure risks being caught flat-footed if a credible alternative emerges or if specific local governance failures — on housing, public safety, or service delivery — mobilize swing voters." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Ideologically Monolithic", tag: "avoid", why: "While the Democratic sweep reflects genuine alignment with progressive positions on healthcare and climate, a $113,578 median-income district with high percentages of college-educated professionals and small business owners contains significant populations whose primary concern is economic stability, not ideological purity. Candidates who campaign as though all constituencies want identical progressive positioning on every issue — rather than understanding that some renter constituencies want housing supply expansion while some small business owners want regulatory relief — will miss both persuasion opportunities and coalition-building possibilities." }
