          { name: "Pluralistic Coalition Maintenance Across Racial and Ethnic Lines", tag: "lean-into", why: "MD-HD-21 has no single demographic majority — 34.16% Black, 27.08% White, 24.08% Hispanic/Latino, 10.49% Asian, with ~4% unaccounted for — creating a genuinely pluralistic district where Democratic success depends on sustaining support across all communities simultaneously. Any signal that the Democratic ticket is prioritizing one community over others, or that specific constituencies are invisible to the governing agenda, creates the opening for Republican recruitment. Sustained, visible, differentiated engagement with each community's specific economic and social concerns is the structural requirement for holding all three seats." },
          { name: "Housing Affordability and Renter Economics", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 45.7% renter rate in a $99,088-income district signals a high proportion of young professionals, economically diverse households, and renters by circumstance rather than choice — a constituency whose economic security depends directly on rental market conditions, tenant protections, and pathways to affordable homeownership. Housing costs, eviction protections, and equitable access to credit and down payment assistance are the material policy issues that distinguish this younger, renter-heavy urban district from older, homeowner-dominant suburban and rural constituencies and activate core Democratic support." },
          { name: "Asian Voter Consolidation and Persuadable Swing Bloc", tag: "careful", why: "At 10.49% of the district, Asian voters represent a meaningful swing constituency in a three-seat at-large system where candidate quality and specific policy engagement drive outcome. Asian voters are frequently underestimated by both parties and can swing decisively when offered specific engagement on education quality, professional licensing reciprocity, small business support, and immigration pathways. In a district this diverse, Republican candidates may attempt targeted recruitment of Asian voters by emphasizing business-friendly policies and immigration critique; proactive Democratic engagement prevents that opening." },
          { name: "Taking the Three-Seat Sweep as a Durable Baseline", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 complete Democratic sweep is a moment of maximum vulnerability precisely because it is total — all three seats are now targets for Republican recruitment and investment in 2026 and 2028 cycles. Any incumbent or campaign that treats the sweep as evidence of a safe Democratic lean in this district is making a fundamental strategic error; the absence of Republican representation is an anomaly in Maryland's competitive context, not a new baseline. Republican candidate recruitment improvements alone, without any shift in underlying voter preference, could flip one or more seats if Democratic candidates assume consolidation has already been achieved." },
          { name: "Treating Educational Attainment as a Guarantee of Democratic Support", tag: "avoid", why: "The 44.6% college attainment in MD-HD-21 is significant but does not automatically translate to Democratic durability — college-educated voters have shifted toward Republicans on economic and governance issues in recent cycles, particularly among business owners, professionals, and households concerned with tax policy. In a district with $99k median income and substantial professional and entrepreneurial populations, Republican messaging on economic growth, business regulation, and fiscal responsibility can gain traction without explicit engagement of the college-educated demographic's actual economic concerns. Assuming education level predicts party loyalty without active persuasion work is a path to losing persuadable college-educated voters to Republican alternatives." }
