          { name: "Housing Affordability and Renter-Homeowner Coalition Balance", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 37.2% renters and $123,750 median income, this district is split between professionals who cannot access homeownership in the current market and homeowners anxious about property taxes and neighborhood change. This is not an ideological divide—it is an economic one created by regional housing costs. A representative who addresses both renters' cost pressures and homeowners' investment concerns simultaneously, rather than picking a side, consolidates the 67% majority into a durable coalition. Any candidate who privileges rent control rhetoric without addressing homeowner property tax relief or school investment will find the 37.2% renter constituency mobilizable by a challenger who promises balanced housing policy." },
          { name: "Governance Competence and Service Delivery in a High-Income, High-Education District", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 50.5% college attainment and $123,750 median income, MD-33A voters are sophisticated about governance and evaluate candidates on measurable outcomes: school quality, transportation efficiency, public safety, and community amenities. Generic partisan messaging significantly underperforms in districts this affluent; voters want specific evidence of competence and delivery. A representative who can point to concrete accomplishments—improved transit outcomes, higher school performance, reduced crime rates, or specific community improvements—consolidates confidence. One who defaults to partisan positioning without demonstrating hands-on constituent service becomes vulnerable to a challenger offering superior execution." },
          { name: "Demographic Change and Cohort Turnover Among Younger Professionals", tag: "careful", why: "The 37.2% renter rate in a district with $123,750 median income suggests either recent in-migration of younger professionals or a significant cohort priced out of homeownership despite high incomes. This is a potential electoral vulnerability if younger renters perceive their representative as prioritizing homeowner concerns over housing access. Simultaneously, if this cohort gains homeownership over the next election cycle, their priorities may shift toward property tax and school investment. A representative needs to monitor how this group's housing status is changing and ensure their messaging evolves as renters transition to ownership—or risks being perceived as out of touch with the constituency's actual circumstances." },
          { name: "Assuming 67% is a Safe Floor Rather Than an Active Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "In a college-educated, high-income district with 37.2% renter volatility and active engagement, a 67% partisan result is a reflection of current candidate-constituency alignment, not an unshakeable base. If the incumbent takes the majority as given and neglects constituent service, or if a well-resourced challenger emerges offering superior competence or more balanced renter-homeowner messaging, this margin can compress quickly. The 33% Republican result is floor enough to sustain a competitive challenger if Democrats treat this as safe rather than durable." },
          { name: "Treating Renters and Homeowners as Separate Constituencies Rather Than Overlapping Families", tag: "avoid", why: "The instinct to pit renters against homeowners—offering aggressive rent control rhetoric to mobilize one group—misreads this district's actual composition. At $123,750 income, many households contain both renters and homeowners, either as dual-income couples where one is still renting or as families with multiple housing situations. A representative who frames housing policy as a zero-sum choice between tenant protection and property tax relief actively divides their own coalition. Messaging that positions housing affordability and homeowner stability as compatible goals—not competing ones—is both more authentic to how these voters actually live and more electorally durable." }
