          { name: "Non-College-Educated Working-Class Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "While 32.9% college-attainment is solid, 67.1% of adults lack a bachelor's degree — a working-class majority in the trades, service sector, and non-professional occupations whose wage stagnation, healthcare costs, and economic opportunity concerns are distinct from affluent college-educated households. At median age 47.3, many in this group are midcareer with significant economic pressure from healthcare costs, childcare, and job-market volatility. An incumbent whose legislative agenda emphasizes homeowner and business-elite priorities while neglecting working-class economic security invites a challenger to organize this constituency around concrete material concerns." },
          { name: "Unexplained 4.5% Demographic Gap and Unaccounted Population", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for 95.5% of MD-HD-37B's population, leaving 4.5% (roughly 1,200-1,500 residents) whose demographic category, national origin, immigration status, and political alignment are unknown. This gap is larger than typical Census under-enumeration and could represent either recent immigrant communities, communities of color not captured in the four reported categories, or other constituencies whose civic participation and political leaning have not yet been activated or measured. Until this population's composition and concerns are understood, it represents both latent electoral opportunity and analytical blind spot." },
          { name: "Treating 100% Republican as a Stable Floor Rather Than Full Consolidation", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 sweep of both at-large seats in MD-HD-37B reflects total Republican consolidation of the current electorate, not necessarily an indestructible partisan alignment. In a 2-seat at-large district where one incumbent can be vulnerable to a challenger campaign targeting renters, working-class households, and the unaccounted population, assuming that 100% translates to a durable majority is a strategic error. The current Republican dominance is real, but it rests on the homeowner majority; any campaign that mobilizes the 23.3% renter constituency and the non-college-educated working-class plurality while maintaining base Democratic turnout has a genuine pathway to victory." },
          { name: "Neglecting Material Economic Concerns in Favor of Abstact Conservative Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "A district with $81,912 median income and 47.3 median age is not primarily motivated by abstract ideological conservatism — it is motivated by economic security, property values, and quality of life for existing households. An incumbent who defaults to boilerplate conservative positioning on taxes and government size while remaining inattentive to concrete concerns (healthcare cost escalation, service-sector wage pressure, childcare affordability, aging-parent care costs) invites a challenger to outflank them by offering specific, material policy remedies. This is a pragmatic, affluent, educated constituency; they evaluate on delivery, not principle." }
