          { name: "Affluent Urban/Suburban Livability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The combination of $100,372 median income, 46.8% renter rate, and 37.7 median age describes professionals for whom walkability, public transit, housing affordability, and service responsiveness determine quality of life. Unlike rural or exurban constituencies, this demographic evaluates state policy through the lens of whether it supports or undermines the urban/suburban livability they have chosen. Democratic advantage is structural here, but only if state investment in housing, transit, and urban infrastructure is visible and effective." },
          { name: "Government Efficiency and Service Delivery", tag: "careful", why: "High-income professionals with resources to relocate or switch allegiances are acutely sensitive to perceived government inefficiency, cost overruns, and regulatory burden. While Democrats have a 22-point margin, any sustained narrative about failed implementation, wasteful spending, or bureaucratic incompetence creates an opening for a challenger to argue that effective management—rather than partisan ideology—should determine voter choice. This constituency will abandon a party it perceives as incompetent, regardless of other alignment." },
          { name: "Treating 61% as a Safe Majority Without Consolidating Base Enthusiasm", tag: "avoid", why: "A 22-point margin is commanding in a swing state context, but not in a district this affluent and educated where swing voters are informed, engaged, and capable of evaluating on competence rather than party. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as secure without active investment in base turnout, education delivery, and service quality will find that margin erode rapidly in a competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Positioning as the Party of Fiscal Restraint in an Affluent District", tag: "avoid", why: "High-income urban professionals do not generally elect Republicans based on tax-cutting rhetoric; they elect Democrats who promise good schools, responsive government, and livable neighborhoods, and they will defect if those promises are not kept. Any Democratic candidate who tries to out-conservative the Republican on spending or taxes will alienate the base without winning swing voters, and any Republican who emphasizes tax cuts over service quality will struggle to compete in a district this educated and affluent." }
