          { name: "Treating 58% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 58-42 result in an affluent, college-educated suburban district is competitive by state standards, not a secure hold. The 42% opposition likely includes college-educated professionals and younger households evaluating on education policy, environmental protection, and responsive governance — constituencies that can organize quickly if they perceive the incumbent is misaligned with community priorities. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 58% represents a comfortable margin rather than a working plurality will find the opposition building credible alternatives." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Hispanic and Asian Communities", tag: "avoid", why: "At 8.06% Hispanic and 4.63% Asian, MD-SD-4 has meaningful immigrant and ethnic-minority communities whose economic mobility concerns, educational advancement expectations, and civic engagement are real constituent needs. In an affluent, college-educated district where the Democratic opposition is strong, these communities represent both genuine constituent service obligations and swing voters whose participation patterns can shift a competitive margin. Treating this district as though it is a white homeowner majority in need of no outreach to immigrant communities is both a governance failure and an electoral vulnerability." }
