          { name: "College-Educated Professional Workforce & Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 40.4% college-educated, MD-SD-5 has a higher concentration of professionals than most districts—and college-educated voters in affluent suburbs have demonstrated willingness to cross party lines on issues like education quality, healthcare access, and professional credential recognition. Engaging this constituency on workforce development, small-business support, and the regulatory environment for professional services builds credibility and prevents Democratic challengers from consolidating college-educated opposition around alternative governance competence." },
          { name: "Demographic Diversity Integration & Economic Inclusion", tag: "careful", why: "The ~8.93% demographic gap in an affluent suburban district likely represents economically integrated Asian American, South Asian, and other professional communities whose members are homeowners, business owners, and professionals with interests aligned with the majority. However, these communities can experience political alienation if messaging appears to exclude them or if specific professional or business obstacles go unaddressed—licensing reciprocity, anti-discrimination enforcement, or targeted small-business support. Treating this segment as economically integrated participants rather than minority interest groups strengthens both representation and electoral durability." },
          { name: "Overestimating Margin Stability in a Shifting Suburban Environment", tag: "avoid", why: "A 65% result in an affluent, college-educated suburban district is not automatically stable; college-educated suburban constituencies have shifted 8-10 points in recent cycles when Democratic messaging on healthcare, education, or environmental governance appears more credible than Republican alternatives. Any candidate who assumes the 65% is a floor rather than the current coalition alignment and neglects to actively engage on the issues college-educated professionals evaluate—education funding, healthcare policy, professional workforce development—risks allowing incremental erosion that becomes structural in the next competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Assuming Demographic Homogeneity or Overlooking the ~8.93% Gap", tag: "avoid", why: "While smaller than in rural districts, the ~8.93% demographic gap represents economically integrated professional communities in an affluent context where visibility and inclusion matter for both governance and electoral durability. A representative whose messaging, constituent engagement, or policy record ignores or appears dismissive of these communities risks allowing them to organize around an alternative candidate, particularly if Democratic messaging emphasizes inclusion and anti-discrimination enforcement more credibly. In a 65-35 district, losing even 2-3 points among this integrated but distinct demographic segment is strategically consequential." }
