          { name: "Assuming the Demographic Gap Is Politically Inert", tag: "avoid", why: "The 5.5% demographic gap between the four reported Census categories and 100% is not a rounding error in a district this competitive — it represents a real population segment whose political participation, candidate preference, and turnout behavior directly affects the outcome. In Maryland, this gap likely includes Latino voters, Asian Americans, multiracial residents, or other communities whose economic interests and electoral organizing capacity can shift a close race. Treating this segment as demographically insignificant or assuming their political preferences are fixed rather than engaged with specifically is a failure to recognize a swing constituency. An opposition campaign that mobilizes these communities around economic opportunity, healthcare access, or perceived neglect can meaningfully narrow the Republican margin." }
