          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as a Governing Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "Jennings' unopposed victory in 2024 reflects the current partisan alignment of this suburban district, not an absence of electoral vulnerability. Unopposed victories can generate complacency; a representative who campaigns as though 100% support is stable rather than contingent on continuous constituent service and policy alignment is setting up for a credible challenge if partisan dynamics shift or if an opponent emerges who better articulates the district's material concerns. The absence of an opponent in 2024 should signal the importance of maintaining active constituent engagement, not permission to govern on autopilot." },
          { name: "Assuming Partisan Loyalty Across All Subgroups", tag: "avoid", why: "MD-SD-7's unopposed result should not be interpreted as uniform partisan commitment across the affluent homeowner majority, the college-educated professional class, minority communities, and renters. Educated suburban voters, in particular, are capable of ticket-splitting and shifting allegiance when they perceive policy misalignment with their priorities — tax policy, healthcare access, education funding, and environmental regulation being the most salient. Any candidate who assumes the district's 2024 unopposed margin is durable without actively building relationships across all these constituencies is vulnerable to a challenger who takes these communities seriously and offers a more compelling case for their material interests." }
