          "Engage the 9.19% Asian constituency with specificity around professional workforce concerns, business development support, and the particular economic mobility pathways that matter to Asian professionals and entrepreneurs in this income and education bracket—not as a demographic category to be managed, but as a constituent community whose success reflects on district economic vitality.",
          "Address the 35.3% renter constituency directly: housing affordability, tenant protections, and the cost-of-living pressures that affect younger and working-class renters are not secondary concerns in a district where more than one-third of households rent. A candidate who offers concrete solutions on housing costs and renter rights consolidates younger adult support and differentiates from opposition that may assume homeowner preferences define the district.",
          "Protect the 66% not by taking it for granted, but by understanding that it reflects active coalition work—Black voter turnout and engagement, Asian professional support, renter community mobilization, and educated homeowner confidence are each earned, not inherited. Any challenger should assume the incumbent has institutional relationships with each community, and any incumbent should recognize that losing focus on any of these groups creates opening for a credible alternative."
