        issues: [
          { name: "Black Community Economic Security & Service Delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 53.51% of the district, Black constituents form the demographic majority—yet the 79% Democratic margin suggests this majority's turnout and engagement are the foundation of the seat's safety. With a median household income of $96,025 (above state averages), this is a community of homeowners and professionals whose economic concerns are quality-of-life issues: education quality, healthcare access, small business lending, and whether government investment in majority-Black neighborhoods matches investment in whiter areas. The incumbent won with 79% because Black constituents believed representation was delivering; maintaining this margin requires demonstrating that investment and advocacy continue at the neighborhood level." },
          { name: "Upper-Income Professional & College-Educated Constituency Retention", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 42.1% college attainment and $96,025 median household income, MD-SD-10 is a district of educated professionals and established homeowners whose electoral behavior is performance-based, not inherited. The 79% result reflects alignment between this constituency's expectations and the incumbent's delivery on schools, economic development, and professional-class quality-of-life issues. Retention of this cohort requires demonstrating that state policy is actively serving their interests—education funding that produces competitive schools, tax policy that protects property values, and economic development that keeps professional jobs in the district. A challenger's pathway here runs through persuading this college-educated, affluent majority that they are underserved." },
          { name: "Renter Community & Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "At 34% of the district, renters represent a constituency substantially larger than typical in majority-Black districts—suggesting significant young adult, student, or working-family renter populations whose housing costs and lease stability are direct quality-of-life concerns. The 79% Democratic margin does not necessarily reflect secured support from renters, who may be more responsive to concrete housing affordability policy (rent control, tenant protections, landlord accountability) than homeowners are. Any challenger with credible housing affordability messaging could mobilize this constituency if the incumbent is perceived as responsive primarily to homeowner interests." },
          { name: "Framing 79% as a Durable Mandate Rather Than a Constituency Actively Engaged", tag: "avoid", why: "A 79% result in a district this affluent and educated is not evidence of passive partisan loyalty—it is evidence of an incumbent who has earned the constituency's confidence through demonstrated delivery. The danger is treating the 79% as a floor rather than as the result of active constituent service, education-focused investment, and economic development that benefits the district's professional majority and Black community simultaneously. Candidates who inherit this seat without maintaining the same intensity of constituent engagement, policy fluency, and community presence will find 79% erodes to 65% or worse against a credible challenger." },
          { name: "Taking the White and Asian Minority Communities for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "At 29.96% white and 5.35% Asian, these communities represent over one-third of the district's electorate—and at 79% Democratic in a majority-Black district, the incumbent is winning significant support from non-Black constituents. The strategic vulnerability is assuming white and Asian voters are locked into opposition because of race; the data shows meaningful percentages supporting the incumbent, likely on grounds of education quality, economic competence, and professional-class policy delivery. Any incumbent or candidate who campaigns to the Black community as if whites and Asians are inherently opposition, or who treats them as obstacles rather than part of the constituency, risks eroding cross-racial support that produced the 79% result." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Baltimore's Professional Majority: 53% Black, $96K Income, 42% College-Educated, and a 79% Democratic Seat Held Through Demonstrable Service to an Affluent, Education-Focused, Cross-Racial Constituency",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "MD-SD-10 is a district of educated, affluent homeowners and professionals whose political alignment with the Democratic incumbent is grounded in performance, not inherited partisan identity. At 53.51% Black, 42.1% college-educated, and $96,025 median household income, this is a constituency that votes on whether government is delivering education quality, neighborhood investment, economic development, and professional-class quality-of-life outcomes—not on abstract ideology. The 79% margin reflects an incumbent who has earned the confidence of Black homeowners and professionals, white and Asian professionals, and educated renters by demonstrating that state policy is actively advancing their material interests. The constituency includes the Black middle class and professional cohorts for whom education quality and property values are inseparable from quality of life, and the 79% result is the evidence that they believe representation is delivering on these fronts.",
          "The strategic vulnerability of this seat is not the 79% margin itself, but the assumption that the margin is durable without active maintenance. At 34% renters, the district includes young adults and working families whose housing costs and lease stability are direct concerns—and whose electoral behavior may be more responsive to concrete housing affordability messaging than to the professional-class education and economic development focus that drives homeowner support. The 21% Republican share is concentrated among constituencies who view the incumbent as insufficiently responsive to their interests or priorities; any candidate seeking to hold or challenge this seat must understand specifically whether opposition is concentrated among white homeowners frustrated by Democratic policy, among conservative Black voters on social issues, or among renters who view the incumbent as focused on homeowner interests over renter concerns. Without this specificity, any candidate treats the 79-21 result as a fixed outcome rather than as the current equilibrium of competing constituencies actively engaged in electoral choice."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with education quality and school investment: at 42.1% college-educated and $96,025 income, this is a constituency for whom K-12 academic performance, college preparedness, and professional-class school reputation are direct household concerns—and the incumbent's credibility on state education funding and accountability is central to the 79% result.",
          "Engage the Black professional and homeowner majority specifically on neighborhood investment, economic development, and whether state policy is actively supporting business creation, property values, and professional-class opportunity in majority-Black communities—not as charity, but as recognition that this constituency drives the district's economy.",
          "Address the 34% renter constituency with concrete housing affordability policy: rent regulation, tenant protections, and whether state-level barriers to affordable housing construction are being actively removed—this cohort is too large to win an election by appealing only to homeowner interests.",
          "Recognize that the 79% includes meaningful support from white and Asian constituents who are voting on education quality and professional-class competence, not on racial identity—any candidate who frames the constituency as 'the Black vote' misses over one-third of the actual electoral coalition and risks fracturing the cross-racial alignment that produced the current margin."
        ]
