          { name: "K-12 Education Quality and Equitable Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "An 85.5% college-educated constituency in a $185k income district is making K-12 school-quality decisions central to housing and family formation choices. School system funding, teacher compensation, STEM and arts program depth, and whether public schools deliver outcomes competitive with private alternatives are the primary governance stakes for professional-class families with children. A state senator who leads on education funding, advocates for teacher compensation that retains talent, and demonstrates visible investment in school system outcomes is directly serving the material concerns of the district's largest demographic cohort." },
          { name: "Housing Affordability and Rental Market Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 33.8% renter rate in a $185k income district signals both younger professionals and immigrant families whose housing costs are constraining their economic mobility and family formation. Rent protection, housing development that serves moderate-income professionals, zoning reform that allows diverse housing supply, and transit connectivity that expands affordable housing geography are the material policy levers. A representative who treats housing as a constituent-centered problem rather than as a market abstraction is serving the 34% of the district whose stakes are most directly in this issue and building relationships with constituencies that feel invisible in homeowner-majority politics." },
          { name: "Asian and Hispanic Community Constituent Service", tag: "careful", why: "At 14% Asian and 9% Hispanic in a professional, immigrant-success district, these communities are visible in the economy and civic life but risk becoming invisible in mainstream political messaging. Credential recognition and professional licensing for immigrant professionals, small business access to capital and permitting, K-12 educational equity in diverse school systems, and whether community organizations feel that the state senator actively engages their specific concerns are the basis of sustainable political relationship. These communities are not persuaded by ethnic targeting but by demonstrable competence and specific attention to their material barriers and opportunities." },
          { name: "Treating a 98-2 Margin as a Durable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 98-2 result reflects a moment of alignment between incumbent performance and constituent expectations in an affluent, educated, overwhelmingly Democratic district—not an immutable electoral foundation. Intra-party competition, fatigue, or governance failures that alienate the professional-class or immigrant-success constituencies can erode this margin rapidly in a district where partisan identity is not the basis of electoral stability. A representative who assumes the margin is safe without continuous attention to whether the district's specific communities feel represented and whether governance problems are being solved is vulnerable to a well-resourced primary challenger." },
          { name: "Treating Asian and Hispanic Communities as Interest Groups Rather Than Core Constituents", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 23% of the population is Asian or Hispanic and concentrated in professional and entrepreneurial occupations, treating these communities as constituencies to manage rather than as core constituents with specific material concerns is both a governance failure and an electoral vulnerability. These communities are not moved by symbolic recognition or ethnic-targeting rhetoric; they evaluate representatives on concrete competence, responsiveness to specific barriers (credential recognition, business access, educational equity), and whether they are heard as constituents. Any representative whose engagement with these communities is secondary to homeowner-majority politics is leaving electoral resources untapped and governing less than a quarter of the district in name only." }
