          { name: "Healthcare Access and Structural Health Equity", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a 77% Black district with $102,614 income and 36% college attainment, constituency expectations around healthcare are sophisticated and grounded in both material access concerns and awareness of structural racial disparities in health outcomes. Healthcare access, maternal mortality reduction, chronic disease management in underserved neighborhoods, and equitable distribution of healthcare resources are not secondary social policy concerns—they are primary economic and health security issues for a constituency whose voting patterns reflect evaluation of whether government is delivering on these specific dimensions. A legislator who speaks to healthcare only in abstract terms, rather than engaging the concrete health equity challenges of this specific district, is ceding ground to a more sophisticated opponent." },
          { name: "Intra-Party Primary Competition and Supermajority Accountability", tag: "careful", why: "The 99% result eliminates general election competition as an accountability mechanism and shifts the arena of real political competition into primary elections—a context where constituent engagement, legislative delivery, and authentic responsiveness to community priorities become the basis on which challengers differentiate themselves. In a supermajority district, any challenger who demonstrates superior fluency on constituent concerns (housing, healthcare, economic opportunity) can capture a significant share of Democratic primary voters without needing to contest the general electorate. An incumbent who assumes the 99% provides stability without continuous constituent engagement is vulnerable to a challenger who shows up with specific, evidence-based solutions to the material concerns driving the supermajority's voting behavior." },
          { name: "Treating 77% Black Population as a Monolithic Voting Bloc", tag: "avoid", why: "While Black constituents are the numerical majority in MD-SD-24, treating them as interchangeable obscures the genuine variation in economic circumstances, age, family structure, and material concerns within that 77%—variation that determines which specific issues activate voter participation and candidate preference. Older Black homeowners, younger Black renters, Black small business owners, and Black workers in different sectors all have distinct economic stakes and policy priorities; a campaign that addresses only generalized 'Black community' concerns rather than these specific constituencies is governing incompletely and ceding space to more granular opposition." },
          { name: "Assuming that 99% Eliminates the Need for Continuous Constituency Responsiveness", tag: "avoid", why: "The 99% result does not reflect automatic loyalty or partisan identity insulation—it reflects current evaluation by this constituency that the incumbent better serves their material interests than available alternatives. In a supermajority district, a primary challenger who demonstrates superior engagement on housing, healthcare, or economic opportunity can shift that 99% significantly downward without needing to contest the general election baseline; the supermajority creates the political space for sophisticated intra-party competition precisely because general election pressure is absent. Any incumbent who responds to a 99% result by reducing constituent engagement or legislative responsiveness to community-identified priorities is inviting a primary challenge from someone who will." }
