          { name: "Housing Affordability & Renter Community Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 17.9% renter rate, renters are a distinct minority but a constituency with material divergence from the homeowner majority. Rising housing costs in districts with $122,391 median income often displace lower-income residents, and renters—who may include younger adults, service workers, and recent immigrants—face specific constraints that homeowners do not. Actively addressing renter affordability and tenant protections is both a policy obligation and a way to consolidate the constituency that is most likely to be alienated by neighborhood development patterns that serve only property-owners." },
          { name: "College-Educated White Voter Retention", tag: "careful", why: "White voters comprise 34.3% of the district, and college education at 31.7% suggests a substantial educated-professional constituency. College-educated white voters nationally show real variance in Democratic loyalty and are sensitive to quality-of-government, education policy, and perceived progressive overreach on local issues like zoning and development. In a safe Democratic seat, the risk is not that this group switches parties en masse but that turnout decline or mild defection reduces the margin in ways that invite future competitive challenge or create an opening for a more conservative primary challenger." },
          { name: "Campaigning as Though the 92% Margin Is Unstable or Threatened", tag: "avoid", why: "A 92-8 result in 2024 is not a competitive margin requiring urgent defensive posturing; it is a supermajority that reflects both demographic alignment and the absence of meaningful electoral competition. Any incumbent who campaigns as though the seat is at risk invites skepticism from constituents who are not experiencing that emergency and raises questions about whether the officeholder understands the actual political context of the district." },
          { name: "Neglecting the Immigrant & Mixed-Race Communities Represented in the Demographic Gap", tag: "avoid", why: "The four reported categories sum to approximately 90.8%, leaving ~9% unaccounted for—a gap that likely represents immigrant communities and mixed-race households. In a high-income urban district with 48.6% Black population and 34.3% white population, the missing 9% could include significant Latino, Asian, immigrant, or mixed-race constituencies whose economic and social concerns (language access, immigration status, economic mobility) can become invisible if the incumbent focuses only on the headline demographic groups. Treating these communities as outside the district's core constituency is both a governance failure and a way to create alienation among voters who are part of the actual constituent base." }
