          { name: "Housing Affordability and Renter Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 34.8% renter rate means that roughly one-third of the district faces direct housing cost pressure and vulnerability to market displacement — a constituency that is large enough to move electoral outcomes when incumbent performance is perceived as inadequate or when an alternative offers more aggressive policy on tenant protection, affordable housing development, and rent stabilization. At median income of $82,233, renters in this district are not primarily low-income households but young professionals and mid-career workers for whom housing cost is a substantial budget constraint. This constituency has demonstrated in other progressive urban districts the capacity to shift electoral outcomes on housing-specific platforms." },
          { name: "Black Community Economic Mobility and Criminal Justice", tag: "careful", why: "At 42.17% of the district, the Black population is the single largest demographic group and includes both middle-class professionals and working-class families with distinct but overlapping policy expectations around criminal justice reform, equitable education funding, wealth-building opportunities, and police accountability. A 97% Democratic result suggests alignment on these issues, but the Black community's support is contingent on measurable progress on racial equity in law enforcement, education, and economic opportunity — not on party label alone. An incumbent who assumes Black support is permanent rather than earned through specific policy delivery and community engagement faces vulnerability if an alternative can credibly claim superior commitment to these outcomes." },
          { name: "Treating 97% as Electoral Safety Rather Than Governance Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "A 97% partisan result in a district with 35% renters, 42% Black population, and 35.1% college attainment can create false confidence that the incumbent has consolidated all relevant constituencies permanently. This reading misses the underlying policy differentiation within that 97% — between renters and homeowners, between young professionals and established mid-career workers, between Black middle-class families and those with fewer economic buffers, and between primary voters and general election voters. An incumbent who campaigns as though 97% represents stable coalition rather than contingent support on specific education, housing, and racial justice outcomes will find that assumption tested when policy implementation disappoints or a credible alternative emerges on education or housing policy." },
          { name: "Neglecting the 4.9% Demographic Gap and Multiracial Community", tag: "avoid", why: "The four reported Census categories account for only 95.09% of the district population, leaving approximately 4.9% unaccounted for — likely multiracial and other ethnic communities. While this gap is smaller than in some districts, it represents a constituency that is not fully visible in standard demographic reporting and whose concerns around identity representation, equitable service delivery, and specific policy attention may be overlooked if the incumbent defaults to treating the district as simply Black, White, Hispanic, and Asian in their reported proportions. Strategic neglect of communities that do not cleanly fit Census categories can create openings for challengers who explicitly center multiracial and non-traditional community identities." }
