          { name: "Treating the 99% as Permission to Govern Without Material Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "The overwhelming Democratic margin is not permission to ignore the constituency's material concerns or to assume passive voter loyalty. In heavily partisan districts, the political risk shifts from electoral challenge to constituent apathy—the slow erosion of participation when voters conclude elected officials are not delivering meaningful improvements in their lives. A senator who assumes the 99% is secure and governs without attending to housing stability, job quality, and public service delivery risks converting a supermajority into a hollow electoral fact." },
          { name: "Neglecting Community Institutions as Primary Engagement Channel", tag: "avoid", why: "In a 74% Black urban district, Black churches, fraternal organizations (Alpha Phi Alpha, Kappa Alpha Psi, Jack and Jill), Black-owned business associations, and community-based nonprofits are the primary civic infrastructure through which African American constituents organize, receive information, and evaluate elected officials. A senator who operates through traditional Democratic Party structures alone while neglecting regular engagement with these institutions will find that civic participation gradually declines and that community-based challengers or alternative organizations fill the void. These are not special interests; they are the operational backbone of community consciousness." }
