          "The 24.9% college attainment rate and $55,428 median income together signal a constituency where economic mobility, job quality, and workforce development are primary quality-of-life concerns—not abstract policy debates. The high renter rate (47.2%) indicates significant housing instability and affordability pressure; rental market costs relative to income determine whether a working-class household can build savings, manage emergencies, or plan for the future. A senator who can articulate specific, measurable progress on tenant protections, affordable housing development, living wage employment in the district, and public service quality (schools, transit, public safety) is consolidating the supermajority. A senator who treats the 99% as permission to govern without attending to these material concerns risks the more dangerous outcome: not electoral loss, but constituent apathy and declining participation—the slow erosion of a supermajority into a hollow victory."
